  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Absence of tooth or teeth due to developmental conditions, not to trauma or extraction:",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Total anodontia—absence of all teeth due",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "to failure in development.",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Partial anodontia—failure in develop-",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "ment of part of the dentition (hypodontia, oligodontia—some teeth missing).",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Edentulous—“without teeth” but",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "primarily due to tooth loss (e.g., end-stage periodontal disease).",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In dogs, premolars or distal molars are the most common missing teeth.",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If a deciduous tooth is missing, its permanent successor will probably not develop as well.",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If a permanent tooth is missing, and the deciduous tooth is still present (persistent), if root structure is stable, that deciduous tooth might stay functional for a long time; lack of permanent tooth should be documented.",
              "pdf_page": 1423,
              "printed_page": 1341,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "DEFINITION": {
